<!-- ================= HERO ================= -->
<section class="hero" id="hero">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">A single-file field guide · real-time graphics</p>
      <h1>Ray <em>Marching</em></h1>
      <p class="lead">A family of rendering techniques that finds surfaces by <b>walking a ray through a field of distance</b> — one exact, safe step at a time. No meshes, no ray tracing: just a function that answers <code>how far</code>, and the patience to keep walking.</p>
      <div class="chips">
        <span class="chip">WebGL · GLSL</span>
        <span class="chip">Signed distance fields</span>
        <span class="chip">60 fps in your browser</span>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-cta">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="#playground">Open the playground ↓</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="#how">How it works</a>
      </div>
      <p class="hero-note">Every demo on this page is computed live on your GPU. No videos, no pre-rendered frames — it's really marching right now.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="gl-frame" id="heroFrame">
      <canvas id="heroCanvas" aria-label="Live ray-marched 3D scene with a sphere, a cube and an octahedron over a wavy floor"></canvas>
      <div class="gl-cap">
        <span class="l"><span class="livedot"></span>live · ray-marched in your browser</span>
        <span>move to steer the camera</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= 01 / 02 · HOW IT WORKS ================= -->
<section class="sec" id="how">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="shead rv">
      <span class="snum">01 · The big idea</span>
      <h2>No triangles. Just a distance.</h2>
      <p class="lead">Classic rendering gives the GPU a mesh — millions of little triangles it tests against. Ray marching inverts the problem: describe the whole scene as a <b>signed distance field</b>, a function <span class="mono">f(p)</span> that tells you, for any point <span class="mono">p</span>, how far you are from the nearest surface (positive outside, negative inside). The surface lives wherever <span class="mono">f(p) = 0</span>.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid-2">
      <figure class="fig rv" role="group" aria-label="Diagram of the marching process">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 560 300" role="img" aria-label="A ray from a camera is marched step by step toward a wobbly surface until it hits">
          <g stroke="var(--line)" opacity=".55">
            <line x1="40" y1="20" x2="40" y2="280"/><line x1="100" y1="20" x2="100" y2="280"/>
            <line x1="160" y1="20" x2="160" y2="280"/><line x1="220" y1="20" x2="220" y2="280"/>
            <line x1="280" y1="20" x2="280" y2="280"/><line x1="340" y1="20" x2="340" y2="280"/>
            <line x1="400" y1="20" x2="400" y2="280"/><line x1="460" y1="20" x2="460" y2="280"/>
            <line x1="520" y1="20" x2="520" y2="280"/>
            <line x1="20" y1="60" x2="540" y2="60"/><line x1="20" y1="120" x2="540" y2="120"/>
            <line x1="20" y1="180" x2="540" y2="180"/><line x1="20" y1="240" x2="540" y2="240"/>
          </g>
          <path d="M 420 60 C 480 55 520 95 515 140 C 510 190 470 225 420 222 C 372 219 340 185 345 140 C 350 96 372 64 420 60 Z"
                fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--line2)" class="d-blob"/>
          <text class="d-lbl" x="414" y="150">surface: f(p)=0</text>
          <rect x="48" y="128" width="46" height="44" rx="9" class="d-cam"/>
          <circle cx="103" cy="150" r="9" class="d-lens"/>
          <text class="d-lbl" x="52" y="194">camera</text>
          <line x1="114" y1="150" x2="348" y2="128.5" class="d-ray"/>
          <circle cx="160" cy="145.7" r="3.4" class="d-dot"/>
          <circle cx="212" cy="141" r="3.4" class="d-dot"/>
          <circle cx="256" cy="136.9" r="3.4" class="d-dot"/>
          <circle cx="292" cy="133.6" r="3.4" class="d-dot"/>
          <circle cx="318" cy="131.2" r="3.4" class="d-dot"/>
          <circle cx="336" cy="129.5" r="3.4" class="d-dot"/>
          <circle cx="346" cy="128.7" r="3.4" class="d-dot"/>
          <circle cx="352" cy="128" r="5" class="d-hitcore"/>
          <circle cx="352" cy="128" r="9" class="d-hitring"/>
          <text class="d-lbl hot" x="300" y="106">hit · d ≈ 0</text>
          <text class="d-lbl" x="126" y="170">ro</text>
          <text class="d-lbl" x="216" y="158">rd · each step += f(p)</text>
        </svg>
        <div class="fig-cap"><span>fig. 01 — the walk</span><span>step sizes shrink as f(p) → 0</span></div>
      </figure>

      <div class="code rv d1">
        <div class="code-bar">
          <span class="dot r"></span><span class="dot y"></span><span class="dot g"></span>
          <span class="code-name">core_walk.glsl</span>
          <button class="copy-btn" type="button">Copy</button>
        </div>
<pre><span class="c">// One ray per pixel: build a camera, then walk it to the surface</span>
<span class="t">vec3</span> ro = <span class="f">vec3</span>(<span class="n">0.0</span>, <span class="n">0.6</span>, <span class="n">3.0</span>);  <span class="c">// camera position</span>
<span class="t">vec3</span> ta = <span class="f">vec3</span>(<span class="n">0.0</span>, <span class="n">0.3</span>, <span class="n">0.0</span>);  <span class="c">// where we're aiming</span>
<span class="t">vec3</span> fw = <span class="f">normalize</span>(ta - ro);
<span class="t">vec3</span> rt = <span class="f">normalize</span>(<span class="f">cross</span>(fw, <span class="f">vec3</span>(<span class="n">0.0</span>,<span class="n">1.0</span>,<span class="n">0.0</span>)));
<span class="t">vec3</span> up = <span class="f">cross</span>(rt, fw);
<span class="t">vec3</span> rd = <span class="f">normalize</span>(uv.x * rt + uv.y * up + <span class="n">1.7</span> * fw);

<span class="t">float</span> t = <span class="n">0.0</span>;
<span class="k">for</span> (<span class="t">int</span> i = <span class="n">0</span>; i <span class="n">&lt;</span> <span class="n">128</span>; i++) {
    <span class="t">vec3</span>  p = ro + rd * t;   <span class="c">// probe point along the ray</span>
    <span class="t">float</span> d = <span class="f">sceneSDF</span>(p); <span class="c">// signed distance to the surface</span>

    <span class="k">if</span> (d <span class="n">&lt;</span> <span class="n">0.001</span>) <span class="k">break</span>;  <span class="c">// hit — p is on the surface</span>
    t += d;               <span class="c">// exact step: can never skip the surface</span>
}</pre>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="shead rv" style="margin-top:76px">
      <span class="snum">02 · The walk</span>
      <h2>Four moves, repeated a hundred times</h2>
      <p class="lead">The whole trick fits in a dozen lines. Each of these four moves happens per pixel, per frame — hundreds of millions of times a second on your GPU.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="steps">
      <div class="card rv"><div class="stepnum">1</div><h3>Aim a ray</h3><p>Every pixel spawns a ray from the camera. This part is the classic per-pixel camera — nothing special. The magic starts after this line.</p></div>
      <div class="card rv d1"><div class="stepnum">2</div><h3>Query the field</h3><p>Evaluate <span class="mono">f(p)</span> at the probe point. A positive number means “you're outside, this far” — and it's an <b>exact</b> lower bound, not an estimate.</p></div>
      <div class="card rv d2"><div class="stepnum">3</div><h3>Step — safely</h3><p>Advance the ray by exactly <span class="mono">d</span>. Because <span class="mono">d</span> is a true distance, the new point can never cross the surface. No missed hits, no tunnelling, ever.</p></div>
      <div class="card rv d3"><div class="stepnum">4</div><h3>Converge &amp; shade</h3><p>Repeat until <span class="mono">d ≈ 0</span>. Then estimate a normal from the field, march shadows toward the light, light the point, and write the pixel.</p></div>
    </div>

    <div class="code rv" style="margin-top:34px">
      <div class="code-bar">
        <span class="dot r"></span><span class="dot y"></span><span class="dot g"></span>
        <span class="code-name">distance_fields.glsl</span>
        <button class="copy-btn" type="button">Copy</button>
      </div>
<pre><span class="c">// A signed distance field (SDF): the entire "mesh" is this function.</span>
<span class="c">// f(p) &gt; 0 → outside · f(p) &lt; 0 → inside · f(p) = 0 → the surface</span>

<span class="t">float</span> <span class="f">sdSphere</span>(<span class="t">vec3</span> p, <span class="t">float</span> r) { <span class="k">return</span> <span class="f">length</span>(p) - r; }

<span class="t">float</span> <span class="f">sdBox</span>(<span class="t">vec3</span> p, <span class="t">vec3</span> b) {
    <span class="t">vec3</span> q = <span class="f">abs</span>(p) - b;
    <span class="k">return</span> <span class="f">length</span>(<span class="f">max</span>(q, <span class="n">0.0</span>)) + <span class="f">min</span>(<span class="f">max</span>(q.x, <span class="f">max</span>(q.y, q.z)), <span class="n">0.0</span>);
}

<span class="c">// Boolean composition — this is how you model with math</span>
<span class="t">float</span> <span class="f">unionSDF</span>(<span class="t">float</span> a, <span class="t">float</span> b)       { <span class="k">return</span> <span class="f">min</span>(a, b); }
<span class="t">float</span> <span class="f">intersectSDF</span>(<span class="t">float</span> a, <span class="t">float</span> b) { <span class="k">return</span> <span class="f">max</span>(a, b); }

<span class="c">// Smooth union — the operator behind every metaball effect</span>
<span class="t">float</span> <span class="f">smin</span>(<span class="t">float</span> a, <span class="t">float</span> b, <span class="t">float</span> k) {
    <span class="t">float</span> h = <span class="f">clamp</span>(<span class="n">0.5</span> + <span class="n">0.5</span> * (b - a) / k, <span class="n">0.0</span>, <span class="n">1.0</span>);
    <span class="k">return</span> <span class="f">mix</span>(b, a, h) - k * h * (<span class="n">1.0</span> - h);
}</pre>
    </div>

    <div class="note rv d1">
      <span class="note-k">Why it matters</span>
      <p>A single SDF function can describe a 100-million-polygon scene in a few hundred bytes. That's why ray marching powers procedural worlds on Shadertoy, metaball and goo effects in games, and even accelerated path tracing — the whole scene is just <em>math you can differentiate</em>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= 03 · SHADING ================= -->
<section class="sec" id="shading">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="shead rv">
      <span class="snum">03 · From a hit to a pixel</span>
      <h2>A surface with no data is still shadeable</h2>
      <p class="lead">When the walk converges you hold a bare point — no mesh, no normals, no UVs. The beautiful part: the SDF gives you everything back, for free. Differentiate it for normals, re-march it for shadows, and light it like anything.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="steps" style="margin-bottom:34px">
      <div class="card rv"><span class="tag">Normal estimation</span><h3>Normals without meshes</h3><p>Sample the SDF on a tiny tetrahedron around the hit and normalize the result. A four-call gradient that's smooth to the last pixel — no faceting, no stored data.</p></div>
      <div class="card rv d1"><span class="tag">Lighting model</span><h3>Light that reads the field</h3><p>Diffuse, bounce light and a fresnel rim all come straight from the normal and the ray direction. Materials become palette choices, not texture files.</p></div>
      <div class="card rv d2"><span class="tag">Soft shadows</span><h3>March toward the light</h3><p>Fire a second ray at the light and accumulate how open the path is. The same SDF gives you penumbra softness instead of hard, binary cut-offs.</p></div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid-2">
      <div class="code rv">
        <div class="code-bar">
          <span class="dot r"></span><span class="dot y"></span><span class="dot g"></span>
          <span class="code-name">normals_lighting.glsl</span>
          <button class="copy-btn" type="button">Copy</button>
        </div>
<pre><span class="c">// No stored normals: estimate them from the SDF with a tetrahedron</span>
<span class="t">vec3</span> <span class="f">calcNormal</span>(<span class="t">vec3</span> p) {
    <span class="k">const</span> <span class="t">vec2</span> e = <span class="t">vec2</span>(<span class="n">0.001</span>, <span class="n">0.0</span>);
    <span class="k">return</span> <span class="f">normalize</span>(<span class="t">vec3</span>(
        <span class="f">sd</span>(p + e.xyy) - <span class="f">sd</span>(p - e.xyy),
        <span class="f">sd</span>(p + e.yxy) - <span class="f">sd</span>(p - e.yxy),
        <span class="f">sd</span>(p + e.yyx) - <span class="f">sd</span>(p - e.yyx)));
}

<span class="t">vec3</span> <span class="f">shade</span>(<span class="t">vec3</span> p, <span class="t">vec3</span> n, <span class="t">vec3</span> rd) {
    <span class="t">vec3</span>  lig = <span class="f">normalize</span>(<span class="t">vec3</span>(<span class="n">0.7</span>, <span class="n">0.9</span>, -<span class="n">0.4</span>));
    <span class="t">float</span> dif = <span class="f">clamp</span>(<span class="f">dot</span>(n, lig), <span class="n">0.0</span>, <span class="n">1.0</span>);
    <span class="t">float</span> bac = <span class="f">clamp</span>(<span class="f">dot</span>(n, -lig), <span class="n">0.0</span>, <span class="n">1.0</span>);
    <span class="t">float</span> fre = <span class="f">pow</span>(<span class="f">clamp</span>(<span class="n">1.0</span> + <span class="f">dot</span>(n, rd), <span class="n">0.0</span>, <span class="n">1.0</span>), <span class="n">3.0</span>);
    <span class="t">vec3</span>  alb = <span class="t">vec3</span>(<span class="n">0.9</span>, <span class="n">0.45</span>, <span class="n">0.3</span>);

    <span class="k">return</span> alb * (<span class="n">0.6</span> * dif + <span class="n">0.25</span> * bac + <span class="n">0.35</span>)     <span class="c">// diffuse · bounce · ambient</span>
         + <span class="t">vec3</span>(<span class="n">1.0</span>)  * fre * <span class="n">0.25</span>                    <span class="c">// fresnel rim</span>
         + <span class="t">vec3</span>(<span class="n">1.0</span>,<span class="n">0.9</span>,<span class="n">0.8</span>) * <span class="f">pow</span>(dif, <span class="n">64.0</span>);        <span class="c">// specular</span>
}</pre>
        <div class="code-caption"><b>The tetrahedron trick:</b> three axis-aligned sample pairs give a full 3D gradient in 6 evaluations, cheaper and smoother than 8.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="code rv d1">
        <div class="code-bar">
          <span class="dot r"></span><span class="dot y"></span><span class="dot g"></span>
          <span class="code-name">soft_shadow.glsl</span>
          <button class="copy-btn" type="button">Copy</button>
        </div>
<pre><span class="c">// March a second ray toward the light; never sample the SDF directly</span>
<span class="t">float</span> <span class="f">softShadow</span>(<span class="t">vec3</span> ro, <span class="t">vec3</span> rd) {
    <span class="t">float</span> res = <span class="n">1.0</span>;
    <span class="t">float</span> t   = <span class="n">0.02</span>;
    <span class="k">for</span> (<span class="t">int</span> i = <span class="n">0</span>; i <span class="n">&lt;</span> <span class="n">32</span>; i++) {
        <span class="t">float</span> h = <span class="f">sd</span>(ro + rd * t);
        res = <span class="f">min</span>(res, <span class="n">10.0</span> * h / t);  <span class="c">// h / t → penumbra softness</span>
        t  += <span class="f">clamp</span>(h, <span class="n">0.02</span>, <span class="n">0.35</span>);
        <span class="k">if</span> (res <span class="n">&lt;</span> <span class="n">0.004</span> || t <span class="n">&gt;</span> <span class="n">12.0</span>) <span class="k">break</span>;
    }
    <span class="k">return</span> <span class="f">clamp</span>(res, <span class="n">0.0</span>, <span class="n">1.0</span>);
}</pre>
        <div class="code-caption"><b>Why it's soft:</b> multiplying distance by <b>1/t</b> turns “how close is an occluder” into “how much light gets through”. It's the same safe-stepping rule, just aimed at the sun.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
